soc: qcom: smp2p-sleepstate: no wakeups while hibernating

The driver (moWerk's 0032) told the ADSP's sensor framework about suspend
only.  While hibernating, the SEE kept raising its sleepstate_see bit
(about twice a second with an accelerometer stream), and each raise, a
hard wakeup, aborted the freeze of the kernel threads ("Freezing remaining
freezable tasks aborted", 2026-11-18).  The awake bit is now cleared for
hibernation too, and no wakeup event made meanwhile: the system powers off
once the image is saved.

Not yet built.  The same effect, by unloading the module (pro1x-sleep),
let the pm_test freezer run pass on the phone.

--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/smp2p-sleepstate.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/smp2p-sleepstate.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@ struct sleepstate {
 	struct qcom_smem_state *state;
 	struct wakeup_source *ws;
 	struct notifier_block nb;
+	bool hibernating;
 };
 
 static int sleepstate_pm_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb,
@@ -37,6 +38,18 @@ static int sleepstate_pm_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb,
 	case PM_POST_SUSPEND:
 		qcom_smem_state_update_bits(ss->state, AWAKE_BIT, AWAKE_BIT);
 		break;
+	/*
+	 * The system powers off once the image is saved, so the SEE's
+	 * wakeups have nothing to wake: they would only abort the freeze.
+	 */
+	case PM_HIBERNATION_PREPARE:
+		WRITE_ONCE(ss->hibernating, true);
+		qcom_smem_state_update_bits(ss->state, AWAKE_BIT, 0);
+		break;
+	case PM_POST_HIBERNATION:
+		qcom_smem_state_update_bits(ss->state, AWAKE_BIT, AWAKE_BIT);
+		WRITE_ONCE(ss->hibernating, false);
+		break;
 	}
 
 	return NOTIFY_DONE;
@@ -46,7 +59,8 @@ static irqreturn_t sleepstate_irq(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct sleepstate *ss = data;
 
-	pm_wakeup_ws_event(ss->ws, 200, true);
+	if (!READ_ONCE(ss->hibernating))
+		pm_wakeup_ws_event(ss->ws, 200, true);
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
